<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · South America</p><h1 class="st-title">Governorate of the Río de la Plata</h1><p class="st-meta">1534 CE – 1617 CE · capital Asunción</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The first Spanish governorate of the River Plate basin, which founded Buenos Aires and Asunción and opened the interior of southern South America.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1534 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> granted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-de-mendoza">Pedro de Mendoza</a> a governorate south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-pizarro">Pizarro</a>'s lands. Mendoza founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a> in 1536, but hunger and attacks forced its abandonment in 1541. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-de-salazar">Juan de Salazar</a> founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asuncion">Asunción</a> in 1537, upriver on the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-paraguay">Paraguay</a> river, and it became the capital of the governorate.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>From <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asuncion">Asunción</a>, governors such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/domingo-martinez-de-irala">Domingo Martínez de Irala</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alvar-nunez-cabeza-de-vaca">Álvar Núñez Cabeza de Vaca</a> ruled a frontier colony built on alliances with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guarani-people">Guaraní</a>, many of whom were bound in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/encomienda">encomiendas</a>, and on mixed marriages that produced a large mestizo population. Expeditions sought a route to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-de-garay">Juan de Garay</a> founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santa-fe-argentina">Santa Fe</a> in 1573 and refounded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a> in 1580. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hernandarias">Hernandarias</a>, the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/criollo-people">Creole</a> governor, promoted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> missions.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1617 the crown split the vast governorate into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/governorate-of-paraguay">Governorate of Paraguay</a>, based at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asuncion">Asunción</a>, and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/governorate-of-buenos-aires">Governorate of Buenos Aires</a>. Both later joined the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/viceroyalty-of-the-rio-de-la-plata">Viceroyalty of the Río de la Plata</a> created in 1776, and Asunción is remembered as the 'mother of cities' for the towns founded from it.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/governorate-of-the-rio-de-la-plata.jpg" alt="Governorate of the Río de la Plata" width="320" height="432"><figcaption>Image: Milenioscuro · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gobernaciones_espa%C3%B1olas_en_Am%C3%A9rica_del_Sur_(1534-1539).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1534 CE – 1617 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Asunción</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Pedro de Mendoza, Domingo Martínez de Irala, Hernandarias</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Roman Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Spanish, Guaraní</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/paraguay">Paraguay</a>, <a href="/countries/argentina">Argentina</a>, <a href="/countries/uruguay">Uruguay</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Governorate of the River Plate, Nueva Andalucía (Mendoza)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1576&amp;focus=governorate-of-the-rio-de-la-plata" aria-label="Governorate of the Río de la Plata on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="3197 3430 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Governorate of the Río de la Plata lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M3517 3686L4910 989M3517 3686L2883 2883M3517 3686L3473 3363M3517 3686L3496 3766"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4910 989h0M2883 2883h0M3473 3363h0M3496 3766h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3517 3686h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3517 3686h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1576&amp;focus=governorate-of-the-rio-de-la-plata">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

